Fix silent loss of files opened from Finder at mpv startup

WHY THIS EXISTS
    input/dnd.c registers two property observers back to back and gates all
    drop handling on `enabled`, which only becomes true when the initial
    "input-builtin-drag-and-drop" event arrives. The initial events for the two
    observers have no guaranteed order, so when "dropped-files" arrives first
    the file is discarded silently -- no log line, no command, no error.

    Finder double-clicks travel this same path (application:openURLs: ->
    mp_input_drop_files), so losing the race loses the open. Because the .app
    bundle runs with --player-operation-mode=pseudo-gui (which implies --idle),
    mpv then sits on the "Drop files or URLs to play here" screen rather than
    playing, intermittently and with nothing in the log to explain it.

    Measured on this machine before the fix: 2 of 5 cold launches failed. The
    failing runs logged "Opening dropped files" but issued zero loadfile
    commands. After the fix: 10 of 10 cold launches played.

    The patch reads the option synchronously before registering either
    observer, so the ordering dependency is gone. The observer still keeps the
    flag current if the option changes at runtime.

WHEN TO DROP THIS PATCH
    When upstream fixes the race itself. build-mpv watches for this and will
    say so; there are two different signals depending on how upstream fixes it.

    1. "already present upstream" (a warning, build continues)
           Upstream landed a change equivalent to this one, so the patch is now
           redundant. Note that `git apply --3way` treats an already-applied
           patch as a successful no-op and exits 0 -- build-mpv reverse-applies
           each patch first specifically to catch this, because otherwise an
           obsolete patch would keep silently "succeeding" forever.

    2. "Failed to apply" (an error, build aborts)
           Upstream reworked this code in some other way. The patch may be
           redundant, or may still be needed in a different form. Do not skip
           it to get the build through.

    Either way, confirm by reading the code:
        cd ~/_runtime/mpv
        git log --oneline HEAD..origin/master -- input/dnd.c
        grep -n 'enabled' input/dnd.c

    The race is fixed upstream if `enabled` is seeded before the observers are
    registered, or if the separate enable flag is gone entirely (e.g. the option
    is queried at drop time, or pending drops are buffered until it is known).
    If neither is true, the bug is still there regardless of what happened to
    the surrounding code.

WHAT TO CHANGE THEN
    Fixed upstream      -> delete this file. build-mpv logs "No local patches
                           to apply" when the directory is empty; nothing else
                           needs editing.

    Still broken, but
    the code moved      -> rebase it. Edit ~/_runtime/mpv/input/dnd.c by hand,
                           then regenerate the body:
                             git diff HEAD -- input/dnd.c
                           and re-add this header above it. Diff against HEAD,
                           not with a bare `git diff`: `git apply --3way`
                           implies --index, so after a build the index already
                           holds the patched blob and a plain diff comes out
                           empty or partial.

    Verifying a change  -> the check that matters is cold-launch open, not that
                           mpv builds. Quit mpv, `open -a mpv <file>` on a real
                           media file, and confirm the log has both "Opening
                           dropped files" and "Run command: loadfile". Repeat
                           ~10 times; the bug is intermittent, so a single pass
                           proves nothing.

    Upstream base for this patch: dd5d17d328 (blob b03b2bb486)
    Reported upstream: no, deliberately. Carried locally instead. Upstream will
    reach this on its own schedule; if it does, the "already present upstream"
    warning above is what surfaces it, so nothing here depends on having filed
    a report.

diff --git a/input/dnd.c b/input/dnd.c
index b03b2bb486..ca20bbc9a9 100644
--- a/input/dnd.c
+++ b/input/dnd.c
@@ -111,6 +111,31 @@ static MP_THREAD_VOID mpv_event_loop_fn(void *arg)
     mp_thread_set_name("dnd");
     mpv_handle *mpv = arg;
     bool enabled = false;
+
+    // Seed the flag synchronously, before either observer below is registered.
+    //
+    // Both observers deliver an initial property change event, and their
+    // relative order is unspecified. Letting the "input-builtin-drag-and-drop"
+    // event be the only thing that sets `enabled` is therefore a race: when
+    // "dropped-files" arrives first, the branch below sees enabled == false and
+    // drops the files on the floor. Nothing is logged and no command is issued,
+    // so the failure is completely silent.
+    //
+    // Files opened from the file manager go through this same path, not just
+    // literal drag-and-drop (on macOS: application:openURLs: ->
+    // mp_input_drop_files), so losing the race loses the file the user opened.
+    // With --idle (implied by --player-operation-mode=pseudo-gui, i.e. the
+    // macOS .app bundle) mpv then sits on the idle screen instead of playing,
+    // intermittently and with no diagnostic. Opening the file again usually
+    // works, because by then the flag has been set.
+    //
+    // Reading the property up front removes the ordering dependency entirely;
+    // the observer still keeps the flag current if the option changes later.
+    int builtin_dnd = 0;
+    if (mpv_get_property(mpv, "input-builtin-drag-and-drop", MPV_FORMAT_FLAG,
+                         &builtin_dnd) >= 0)
+        enabled = builtin_dnd;
+
     mpv_observe_property(mpv, 0, "dropped-files", MPV_FORMAT_NODE);
     mpv_observe_property(mpv, 0, "input-builtin-drag-and-drop", MPV_FORMAT_FLAG);
 
